 Cached JPEG data may occupy at most 12 GiB. Before admitting a completed sheet, evict enough existing sheets to remain within that bound. Temporary render output is kept separately and removed on completion or failure. Eviction never deletes source footage or changes job-ticket semantics.
 
-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+Replacement uses 2Q over the resident sheets, tracked as two queues whose bytes together count against the 12 GiB bound:
+
+- **Probationary queue**: FIFO of sheets admitted by a completed render and not yet served from cache.
+- **Protected queue**: LRU of sheets that have been served from cache at least once while resident.
+
+A completed render is admitted at the tail of the probationary queue. A cache hit on a probationary sheet moves it to the most-recent end of the protected queue; a hit on a protected sheet moves it to the most-recent end of that queue. Requests answered with a pending ticket do not count as hits.
+
+When admission would exceed the bound, evict from the head of the probationary queue while that queue holds more than its target share of the budget; otherwise evict from the least-recent end of the protected queue. If the chosen queue is empty, evict from the other. Eviction repeats until the new sheet fits. A long sequential screening therefore cycles through the probationary queue and leaves the protected queue intact while the probationary queue exceeds its target share.
+
+The probationary target share is a configuration value private to the worker. The queues, their order, and the share are replacement bookkeeping and are never exposed outside the preview worker. The external interface, queue concurrency, and disk budget do not depend on this policy.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Eviction policy | 2Q: probationary FIFO plus protected LRU | LRU; frequency-based eviction with aging | Long sequential screenings add many one-use sheets that plain LRU would let push out the comparison sheets editors revisit. Frequency counting with aging protects popular clips but costs counter maintenance and can retain stale favorites after an editor switches projects. 2Q accepts a second queue and a share parameter to keep reused sheets safe from screening scans. |
+| What counts as reuse | A cache hit on a resident sheet | Any repeat request, including requests that share a pending ticket | Concurrent requests for one pending key reflect a single editor's retry loop, not a revisit; only a served-from-cache hit shows a sheet was wanted again. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Deferred
+1. Probationary target share of the 12 GiB budget (tuned against pilot workloads).
+2. Whether queue order is rebuilt or reset when the worker restarts with sheets already on disk.
